WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.520
Speaker 1: Can Democrats win in the fall by&nbsp;
running on maintaining and restoring abortion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.840
rights? We have an early signal that suggests the&nbsp;
answer, at least in some districts, might be yes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.840
This is a super interesting story. Democrats&nbsp;
just won a swing swing district by focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:23.360
on abortion, and that should scare Republicans,&nbsp;
writes Philip Elliot for Time magazine. This is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:32.000
very interesting situation. Now an unexpected echo&nbsp;
to yesterday's column. Democrats may not be toast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.560
in the House. It's a long shot, but there is a&nbsp;
new data point from Tuesday evening that makes it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:42.400
more plausible sending Republicans in Washington&nbsp;
into a late night scramble. How had they gotten a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:48.720
New York special election so wrong? The seat&nbsp;
representing New York's 19th Congressional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.000
District opened in May when Congressman&nbsp;
Delgado was appointed lieutenant governor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:00.000
Obama twice won the Hudson River Valley&nbsp;
District before it pivoted to Trump in 2016&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.520
and Biden in 2020. It's a swing district&nbsp;
with more than 90% of ballots reported.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:11.440
Democratic nominee Pat Ryan was ahead with&nbsp;
52% of the vote, enough to send him to D.C.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.560
through the balance of Delgado's term. Maybe&nbsp;
everything isn't entirely terrible for Democrats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:23.120
Maybe. Question. Both parties nominated mainstream&nbsp;
candidates to fill Delgado's seat. They invested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:32.480
in the race. Abortion rights was the primary topic&nbsp;
for Democrats and the economy for Republicans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.560
In an almost perfect swing district, the&nbsp;
small turnout election favored the Democrat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.520
a county executive from the establishment wing&nbsp;
of the party. This comes on the heels of Kansas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.640
voters rejecting an amendment to the Kansas&nbsp;
Constitution that would have made it easier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:55.600
to further restrict access to abortion. This is&nbsp;
very interesting because the conventional wisdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.880
is it's the economy, stupid. If the perception&nbsp;
is the economy is good, it's good for whoever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.320
is in power. If the perception is the economy is&nbsp;
bad, it's good for whoever is out of power. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:12.080
in a mixed administration, a mixed Congress,&nbsp;
figuring out, well, who's in can have different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:18.320
interpretations. But putting that aside for a&nbsp;
moment. Typically, particularly for Democrats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.840
when they run on something like abortion or&nbsp;
social issues, generally, it's not particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.800
good. But we have a situation here in this. It's&nbsp;
admittedly it's a special election, but it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:34.480
like the perfect swing district. The candidate&nbsp;
who is a Democrat running on abortion rights.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:41.280
One. Is this a sign that maybe November won't&nbsp;
be as bad for Democrats as some are predicting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:48.000
in the House? Well, it's a possibility, and we've&nbsp;
seen it happen to some degree in Michigan. And at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.520
this point in time with, what is it, about nine&nbsp;
weeks until the midterm elections, incredibly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.600
the economy is good. And so you can walk and chew&nbsp;
gum at the same time. The messaging should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:08.720
look at how great the economy is. And also,&nbsp;
these are dangerous infringements on women's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:14.400
rights coming from the Republican Party, coming&nbsp;
from the right wing Supreme Court. And we need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.040
to elect some Democrats in order to try to&nbsp;
reverse that. And so the question becomes, was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:25.120
the overturning of Roe v Wade potentially very bad&nbsp;
for the Republican Party? We don't know the answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.800
yet. And there are many prognosticators saying&nbsp;
it is bad or it's good or it doesn't matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.440
Well, ultimately, these are just predictions. We&nbsp;
won't know until we see the results in November.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:41.680
The right has a playbook that they stick to,&nbsp;
and they are confident that it will always work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:48.320
And the general population's views on a number&nbsp;
of different issues, particularly social issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.520
but some economic issues as well, like should&nbsp;
taxes be used on the rich to help the general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.000
population? The country has moved to the left&nbsp;
on basically every single one of those issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:08.160
And in a just and reasonable situation, you would&nbsp;
think that at a certain point that public belief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:13.600
those public perceptions, those public opinions&nbsp;
would be reflected in the way that the results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.280
shake out now because of gerrymandering and&nbsp;
campaign finance and all these different things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:21.840
voter suppression from from Republicans, all the&nbsp;
techniques that we've talked about that they use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.240
It hasn't happened to the degree that it should&nbsp;
have. Maybe we will actually see that happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.600
in November. So very good news there out of&nbsp;
that New York district. We're going to watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.600
all districts closely and we're going to watch&nbsp;
the polling in the House as we head into November.

